00:01:16.999 --> 00:01:21.090
So we are in Ytterby in Sweden and it’s
a really nice day and we have come to the

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:25.279
Ytterby Quarry, or Ytterby Groover, which
is actually the birthplace for 4 of the elements

00:01:25.279 --> 00:01:30.499
and really the one we are going to talk about
now is this one right here yttrium. It was

00:01:30.499 --> 00:01:35.159
first discovered in this quarry from a really
horrible black lump of mineral which people

00:01:35.159 --> 00:01:41.479
thought was coal. And over a period of 40
years from the late 1700s on, some scientists

00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:46.920
over in Arbo did a lot of work in refining
this material and they found a new earth or

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.239
yttria. Ok this was yttrium and oxygen and
they couldn’t get the oxygen out by using

00:01:51.239 --> 00:01:57.179
traditional reductive methods, metal making
methods like burning with carbon and charcoal.

00:01:57.179 --> 00:02:01.560
So the yttria survived and it was known for
about 40 years before the German scientist

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:06.999
Volla, decided to react the yttria with a
much more reactive element, potassium. The

00:02:06.999 --> 00:02:12.489
potassium wrestled and ripped out the oxygen
and he made yttrium metal for the first time.

00:02:12.489 --> 00:02:16.240
Yttrium its used in a whole range of different
things, it’s used in semi conductors, where

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:21.159
there is yttrium, barium, copper oxides, the
1-2-3 complexes that we know of and it’s

00:02:21.159 --> 00:02:26.219
used for a whole range of applications. It’s
used in laser technologies to make very intense

00:02:26.219 --> 00:02:33.230
pulses of light, for technologies like CDs
and LEDs etc., etc., but a massive, massive

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:34.230
area.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:39.590
Another activity of another use for yttrium
is actually in the use of yttrium oxisulphide

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:44.200
which is used as an additive in glass it makes
glass really tough, like bullet proof glass

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.630
it makes it very shock resistant and very
very resistant towards temperature changes,

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:52.170
so it is a very useful additive in glasses
and glassy materials.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:03:00.469
Make sure you don’t fall over the rock

00:03:00.469 --> 00:03:06.290
So yttrium itself is a really quite nice lustrous
